NAME
       mkfs.xfs - create an XFS file system

SYNOPSIS
       mkfs.xfs [-f] [-L LABEL] [-b size=BYTES] [-q] [-K] [-N] DEVICE

DESCRIPTION
       mkfs.xfs writes a new, empty XFS file system on a partition, logical
       volume or whole disk. XFS is the default file system of RHEL 9: the
       installer uses it for / and /boot. Everything that was on the device
       before is lost.

       XFS can grow later (xfs_growfs, or lvextend -r) while it is mounted,
       but it can never shrink. Labels are at most 12 characters.

       For safety, mkfs.xfs refuses a device that already holds a file
       system or a partition table and tells you to add -f. Check with lsblk
       -f or blkid that you have the right device before you force it.

       Creating the file system does not mount it. Make a mount point, add a
       line to /etc/fstab (by UUID or LABEL) and mount it.

OPTIONS
       -f     Force: overwrite an existing file system or partition table.

       -L LABEL
              Give the file system a label (up to 12 characters), so it can
              be mounted with LABEL=... in /etc/fstab or mount -L.

       -b size=BYTES
              The block size; the default 4096 suits nearly everything.

       -q     Quiet: do not print the summary of the new file system.

       -K     Do not discard (trim) the device's blocks first.

       -N     Dry run: print the settings that would be used, change nothing.

EXAMPLES
       Make an XFS file system on the first partition of /dev/vdb and mount
       it at /data at every boot:

           mkfs.xfs /dev/vdb1
           mkdir /data
           blkid /dev/vdb1
           echo 'UUID=<the UUID shown> /data xfs defaults 0 0' >> /etc/fstab
           mount -a

       Make a labelled XFS file system on a logical volume, replacing an
       old one:

           mkfs.xfs -f -L webdata /dev/vgweb/web

       Read the settings of a mounted XFS file system:

           xfs_info /data

SEE ALSO
       mkfs(8), mke2fs(8), xfs_growfs(8), xfs_info(8), xfs_admin(8), blkid(8),
       mount(8), fstab(5)
